\emph{(i).} For bivectors $X$, $Y$ the product $XY$ has grades $0$, $2$, $4$, its grade-two part is the commutator $	frac12(XY - YX)$ and its grade-four part is $X \wedge Y$, symmetric in $X$ and $Y$; so $	frac12\langle XY + YXangle_{4} = X \wedge Y$. The relative vectors $\gamma_{i}\gamma_{0}$ anticommute pairwise and square to $1$, $I_{4}$ commutes with them and $I_{4}^{2} = -1$, so $(E + I_{4}B)^{2} = E^{2} - B^{2} + I_{4}(EB + BE) = \sum_{i}(E_{i}^{2} - B_{i}^{2}) + 2 igl(\sum_{i}E_{i}B_{i} igr)I_{4}$; hence $\langle I_{4}F^{2}angle_{0} = -2\sum_{i}E_{i}B_{i}$, the form of \claimref{synthesis.triangle-anomaly-in-the-spacetime-algebra-is-the-pseudoscalar-part-of-the-field-square-and-cancels-per-generation}(iv). Reversal changes the sign of a bivector and the order of a product, so it sends $	frac12(XY + YX)$ to itself, and it fixes grade four; the grade involution fixes even elements. An outermorphism preserves the outer product, and on the top grade it is multiplication by the determinant: $g(X) \wedge g(Y) = g(X \wedge Y) = \det(g)\,X \wedge Y$. Summing with the constants $\kappa_{ab}$ gives the three statements for $\Theta$, which is the invariant polynomial $	r$ evaluated on the field square, the four-form of \claimref{synthesis.imported-chern-weil-and-chern-simons-forms} with $k = 2$. The check verifies them on rational bivectors for a reflection, the time reversal, the total inversion, a boost and a rotation.

\emph{(ii).} By clause (1) of \claimref{synthesis.cpt-conjugation-pseudoscalar-rotor-times-complex-conjugation} the grade involution equals $\mathrm{Ad}(I_{6})$, and by clause (4) $C = \mathrm{Ad}(I_{6}) \circ \kappa$ is $-1$ on $\R^{2,4}$ and $\kappa$ fixes $\Cl(2,4)$. The map $-1$ of a six-dimensional space has determinant $+1$, and its outermorphism is $(-1)^{k}$ on grade $k$: the identity on bivectors and on four-vectors. In the convention of clause (5) a field built from vectors is acted on through the vector part, so every bivector field and every four-form built from two of them is fixed. On the colour index $C$ acts, in the same convention, through the Lie algebra automorphism it induces. If $	au$ is an automorphism of $\su(3)$ then $\mathrm{ad}(	au X) = 	au\,\mathrm{ad}(X)\,	au^{-1}$, so $	r(\mathrm{ad}\,	au X\,\mathrm{ad}\,	au Y) = 	r(\mathrm{ad}\,X\,\mathrm{ad}\,Y)$; the trace form of the defining representation is a multiple of this form, $\su(3)$ being simple, so it is preserved, whichever automorphism the conjugation induces (the check verifies it for the outer one, $X \mapsto  ar{X}$, and for an inner one). The trace form is real on $\su(3)$, so the antilinearity of $\kappa$ does not act on $\kappa_{ab}$. Hence $\Theta(CF) = \Theta(F)$. A real-linear flag conjugate $g$ has $\det g = -1$ by clause (3) there, so $g(I_{6}) = -I_{6}$; if it preserves a four-dimensional subspace and has determinant $-1$ on it, (i) applied in that subspace gives $\Theta(gF) = -\Theta(F)$ for $F$ in it. The check verifies on $\Cl(2,4)$ that $\mathrm{Ad}(I_{6})$ is the grade involution, that a reflection and the product of three reflections reverse $I_{6}$, and that a reflection reverses exactly the four-forms containing its direction.

